pybuddy2: Python library to control your i-Buddy USB device
============================================================

Based on pybuddy (http://code.google.com/p/pybuddy/) by Jose.Carlos.Luna, luis.peralta
Fork: ewall 2010, pybuddy2 2026

DESCRIPTION
-----------
Control an i-Buddy (USB angel figure originally for Windows Live Messenger)
from Python: change head color, blink heart, flap wings, swivel body.


WINDOWS SETUP (2026+)
---------------------

1. Install Python 3.10+ and pyusb:
   pip install pyusb

2. Install WinUSB driver for the i-Buddy using Zadig:
   - Download Zadig: https://zadig.akeo.ie/
   - Run as Administrator
   - Options -> List All Devices
   - Select "i-Buddy" (VID 1130, PID 0005)
   - Choose "WinUSB" as driver
   - Click "Install Driver" (do this for both Interface 0 and Interface 1)

3. Install a libusb DLL for pyusb to use:
   - Download from https://libusb.info/ or install libusb-win32
   - Ensure libusb-1.0.dll is in a PATH directory

4. Usage:
   from pybuddylib import iBuddyDevice

   buddy = iBuddyDevice()
   buddy.doColorName(iBuddyDevice.RED, 0.5)
   buddy.doFlap(3, 0.2)
   buddy.doHeartbeat(5, 0.3)


LINUX SETUP
-----------
   apt-get install python-usb    (Debian/Ubuntu)
   yum install pyusb             (Red Hat/Fedora)

   Then just run without any driver changes.


API
---
Colors:      RED, GREEN, BLUE, YELLOW, PURPLE, CYAN, WHITE
Actions:     doColorRGB(r,g,b), doColorName(tuple), doFlap(), doWiggle(),
             doHeartbeat(), doReset()
Controls:    setHeadColors(), setHeart(), setWing(UP|DOWN), setSwivel(LEFT|RIGHT)


KNOWN ISSUES
------------
- On Windows, the HID driver blocks direct USB access. WinUSB driver (via Zadig) is required.
- The daemon (src/pybuddy-daemon.py) is Unix-only (uses fork(), pwd).
- Device PID on tested hardware is 0x0005; older units may use 0x0001 or 0x0002.


